SQL injection: attacker injects malicious SQL through user input. Prevention:

1) Use PreparedStatement (parameterized queries):
// Vulnerable
String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '" + username + "'";
Statement stmt = connection.createStatement();
ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(query);
// username = "admin' OR '1'='1" bypasses authentication

// Secure
String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?";
PreparedStatement pstmt = connection.prepareStatement(query);
pstmt.setString(1, username);
ResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();
// Input properly escaped

2) Use JPA/Hibernate (automatic escaping):
// Vulnerable (native query with concatenation)
String query = "SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.username = '" + username + "'";
Query q = entityManager.createQuery(query);

// Secure (named parameters)
String query = "SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.username = :username";
Query q = entityManager.createQuery(query);
q.setParameter("username", username);

// Or use Spring Data JPA
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
  Optional<User> findByUsername(String username); // Safe

  @Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.username = :username")
  Optional<User> findByUsernameCustom(@Param("username") String username); // Safe
}

3) Input validation:
@PostMapping("/users")
public User createUser(@Valid @RequestBody UserRequest request) {
  // @Valid triggers validation
}

public class UserRequest {
  @Pattern(regexp = "^[a-zA-Z0-9_]{3,20}$")
  private String username;

  @Email
  private String email;
}

4) Principle of least privilege:
// Database user only has SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE on specific tables
// No DROP, ALTER, DELETE on system tables

5) Stored procedures (if needed):
CallableStatement cstmt = connection.prepareCall("{call getUserByUsername(?)}");
cstmt.setString(1, username);

Never: String concatenation in queries. Always: Parameterized queries, ORM, input validation.

XSS: Attacker injects malicious scripts into web pages. For REST APIs serving JSON (not HTML), XSS less common but still possible if data rendered in frontend. Prevention:

1) Content-Type enforcement:
@RestController
public class UserController {
  @GetMapping(value = "/users/{id}", produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
  public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return userService.findById(id);
  }
}
// Ensures JSON response, not HTML

2) Input validation and sanitization:
@PostMapping("/comments")
public Comment createComment(@Valid @RequestBody CommentRequest request) {
  return commentService.create(request);
}

public class CommentRequest {
  @NotBlank
  @Size(max = 500)
  @Pattern(regexp = "^[^<>]*$", message = "HTML tags not allowed")
  private String content;
}

// Or sanitize HTML
@Component
public class HtmlSanitizer {
  public String sanitize(String input) {
    return Jsoup.clean(input, Whitelist.basic());
  }
}

3) Output encoding (if serving HTML):
// Use template engines with auto-escaping
// Thymeleaf: th:text auto-escapes, th:utext doesn't
<p th:text="${comment.content}"></p> <!-- Safe -->
<p th:utext="${comment.content}"></p> <!-- Unsafe -->

4) Content Security Policy headers:
@Configuration
public class SecurityConfig {
  @Bean
  public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) {
    http.headers()
      .contentSecurityPolicy("default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'");
    return http.build();
  }
}

5) X-XSS-Protection header:
http.headers()
  .xssProtection()
  .and()
  .contentTypeOptions();

6) CORS configuration (prevent unauthorized domains):
@Configuration
public class CorsConfig {
  @Bean
  public WebMvcConfigurer corsConfigurer() {
    return new WebMvcConfigurer() {
      @Override
      public void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {
        registry.addMapping("/api/**")
          .allowedOrigins("https://trusted-domain.com")
          .allowedMethods("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE");
      }
    };
  }
}

7) Never reflect user input directly:
// Vulnerable
@GetMapping("/search")
public String search(@RequestParam String query) {
  return "Search results for: " + query; // If rendered as HTML, XSS possible
}

// Secure
@GetMapping("/search")
public SearchResponse search(@RequestParam String query) {
  return new SearchResponse(sanitize(query), results);
}

Best practice: Treat all user input as untrusted, validate input, encode output, use framework protections.
